## Python REPL specification

{# Deduped against system_prompt.jinja2, which owns the output-format rules (code only, executed
   verbatim, no fences, prose in comments) and renders directly above this section. #}
- Your response should contain Python code.
- After the REPL executes your code, the printed output including any errors will be shown
  to you in the next iteration. It is NOT shown to the caller.
- Printed output will be truncated if it is too large.
- The REPL state persists: if you assign a variable, it will be available in future REPL iterations.
{# `exec_timeout` and `allow_timeout_pragma` are injected at render time (like `allow_raise`),
   never baked: the REPL enforces both, so a stale value would promise the agent time it does not
   get — or an override the REPL ignores. With the pragma off and no bound there is nothing to
   say, so the bullet disappears entirely rather than rendering an empty statement. The bound is
   spelled out in each branch instead of factored out because a mid-line `{% endif %}` at a line
   END would eat the newline after it (trim_blocks) and run this bullet into the next one. #}
{% if allow_timeout_pragma %}
- Your code is executed {% if exec_timeout is not none %}under a timeout of {{ exec_timeout }} seconds. If your code is expected to take longer, change{% else %}without a timeout. If your code risks running forever, set{% endif %} the timeout
  by writing a pragma comment `# timeout: T` on its own line at the top before your code, where `T` is a positive number
  (e.g. `5.0`, `60`, `300`) indicating the number of seconds.
{% elif exec_timeout is not none %}
- Your code is executed under a timeout of {{ exec_timeout }} seconds.
{% endif %}
{# Frame `return` around what it delivers AND scope it to "no next step remains" — not around when it
   is forbidden. Two findings drive this wording:
   - Prohibition-only phrasing ("do NOT return unless ...") cost ~2 turns/run on a one-turn task
     (gpt-5-mini, 12 trials/arm — 5/12 runs took >1 turn at avg 2.83, vs 0/12 at avg 1.00). So the
     primary bullet stays delivery-framed with no "do not return until ..." prohibition.
   - Framing completion as "everything is done" is ambiguous when one invoke covers a *sequence* of
     steps: the agent reads finishing the current step as finishing everything and returns early,
     abandoning the rest. Framing it forward-looking ("no next step remains") removes that read. On a
     task-framed, multi-step sequence with NO worked example (recall-style, 26 items), gpt-5-mini with
     no completion guard completed 0/6 runs under the prior "task completion" wording and 6/6 under
     this wording. It does NOT regress the single-step exits: one-turn and two-turn turn-counts are
     identical between the two wordings across all six models (gpt-5-mini, gpt-5.4-nano, gpt-5.4-mini,
     gpt-5.6-luna, gpt-5.4, gpt-5.6-sol) — see tests/test_return_instruction_integration.py.
   Measured against jaz core b38a66a6; the b38a66a6 -> develop return-path diff is behavior-preserving
   for these numbers (the only non-refactor change is a `max_failures` off-by-one on ReturnType /
   ValidateReturn that no measured run exercised — all used the default `max_failures=None`). #}
- `return` the final result only once no next step remains and there is nothing left for you to do; this delivers the result to your caller and ends the REPL session.
  Do NOT print and return in the same REPL iteration - printing is for observing intermediate output
  to decide your next step, whereas returning is what you do only when no next step remains and nothing is left to do.
{% if allow_raise %}
- If what you were asked is impossible, use a `raise` statement to raise an exception explaining why:
  `raise Exception("<reason why it is impossible>")`.
  Executing `raise` immediately ends the REPL session.
{% endif %}
